  watch: [
    {
      title: "FMCG's 3.65% is a Marico day, and the sector is still down 19% this year",
      detail: "Nifty FMCG rose 3.65% to 45,580, its best session in months, and Marico alone did a large share of it at +8.77% on a quarter that genuinely beat — net profit up 31% to Rs 550 crore, EBITDA margin up 260 basis points to 18.2%, domestic volume growth of 10%, and FY27 guidance raised to mid-20s EBITDA growth from around 20%. But read the level: the sector is 19.2% lower over twelve months and is the cheapest large basket on the board, so this is money arriving in what was left behind as crude fell and the possibility of a capital-gains review brought foreign money back. The question for the rest of the week is whether the bid broadens beyond Marico and the four large caps that followed it — Britannia +6.36%, Nestle +4.47%, Tata Consumer +4.29%, HUL +3.40% — or fades once the results flow stops. Watch ITC: it rose 2.82% on a day its own quarterly net profit fell 18.7%."
    },
    {
      title: "SBI beat on every line and fell 1.70% — that is the whole PSU bank problem",
      detail: "State Bank of India reported a 14.28% rise in September-quarter net profit to Rs 23,038 crore and a 14.69% rise in net interest income to Rs 49,143 crore, and the stock fell 1.70% to Rs 970.25. It had risen 2.61% the day before, so this is sell-the-news rather than disappointment — but it left Nifty PSU Bank as the day's worst sector at -1.27%, and it is the second time in a week that the state-owned complex has moved in the opposite direction to the private banks on the same day. The pattern to watch is whether the capital-gains review, if it happens, sends foreign money into the large private banks only. Foreign flows have historically bought HDFC Bank and ICICI Bank and ignored the PSUs, and today's 3.49% and 2.49% against SBI's -1.70% is that trade in miniature."
    },
    {
      title: "Brent at $105.60 has stopped falling, and half of Monday's rally was a crude trade",
      detail: "Brent recovered about 1% to $105.60 a barrel after Monday's 7.6% slide to $104.77, and the effect on the sector table was immediate and complete: Oil & Gas went from +5.95% to roughly flat, Energy from +3.92% to -0.11%, Metal from +3.21% to -0.14%, and PSU Bank from +1.36% to -1.27%. That is what a one-input market looks like. Crude is still up more than 12% for October and the underlying cause of the move — the US-Israeli war with Iran and the talks around it — has not been resolved; a single headline moved the price 7.6% in one session on Monday and 1% back on Tuesday. Watch $105 as the level that keeps this rally alive and $110 as the level that ends it. Nothing in today's 1.58% had anything to do with Indian earnings."
    },
    {
      title: "The capital-gains review is the biggest policy line since the current regime began",
      detail: "Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman said the government would consider requests to review capital gains taxes on foreign investors, after reports that SEBI had recommended scrapping long-term capital gains tax for foreign equity investors. This is not a small technical change: it is the single biggest swing factor in foreign portfolio flows into India, and foreign institutions have already turned buyers — Rs 3,114.68 crore net on Monday, their second straight session of buying, and buyers in four of the past five sessions per BusinessLine. If it happens, the first beneficiaries are the large, liquid, index-heavy private banks and the capital-market names, which is precisely what rose today: BSE Ltd +5.03%, 360 ONE +5.77%, Motilal Oswal +4.73%, MCX +7.69%, Nifty Capital Market +4.46%. If it does not, that entire leg of today's rally unwinds. Nothing is decided — the final call rests with the government."
    },
    {
      title: "The Nifty closed at its high, but the options market is hedging the breakout",
      detail: "The Nifty closed at 23,810.85 — exactly its session high, which is the strongest possible close, and its highest level in nearly two months. It sits 39 points under the 23,850 strike that carries the largest gamma exposure and the call wall, with the 23,800 put wall just below; the index finished the November expiry inside a 50-point band between the two. India VIX rose 1.42% to 15.72 on a 1.58% up-day, which is the tell: options markets are paying up for protection rather than believing the breakout. The next real test is 23,850 — clear it and 24,000 is the round number everyone will trade against; fail and the four-session 4.89% gain becomes the thing that gets sold. Note also that since the 28 October close the Nifty has gained 4.90% while the Midcap 150 has gained 2.02% and the Smallcap 250 just 0.44%: this rally is being carried by large caps."
    }
  ],

